# Responsive Design Rules

These rules are **non-negotiable** for every UI change in this project.
They exist because the assessment is graded as a product, and a layout that
only works on one phone size is not a product.

## Size classes (never platforms)

- Branch on **width**, never on `Platform.isIOS`, `Platform.isAndroid`, or
  device model. Split-screen, foldables, desktop windows and browser resize
  all change width without changing platform.
- Use the four Material 3 window size classes from
  `lib/core/responsive/app_breakpoints.dart`:

  | Class     | Width (dp)   | Typical device              |
  |-----------|--------------|-----------------------------|
  | compact   | `< 600`      | Phone portrait              |
  | medium    | `600–1023`   | Phone landscape / small tab |
  | expanded  | `1024–1439`  | Tablet landscape / laptop  |
  | large     | `≥ 1440`     | Desktop / TV                |

- Prefer `context.windowSize` / `context.responsive(...)` /
  `ResponsiveBuilder` from `lib/core/responsive/`. Do **not** invent local
  breakpoint constants.

## Measure the parent, not the window

- When a widget can appear inside a pane, dialog, side sheet or grid cell,
  measure with `LayoutBuilder` / `ResponsiveBuilder`. A 380dp detail pane on
  a desktop must lay out like a phone.
- `MediaQuery.sizeOf(context)` is fine for page-level decisions
  (how many grid columns, whether to show a side-by-side detail). It is the
  wrong tool for anything that can be nested.

## Cap content width

- Every page body must go through `ResponsiveCenter` (or
  `ResponsiveSliverCenter` inside a `CustomScrollView`).
- Default max width is `AppBreakpoints.maxContentWidth` (720). Use
  `maxWideContentWidth` (1080) only for charts and multi-column grids.
- Never let a list row stretch to the full width of a 27" monitor.

## Spacing and touch

- Use `AppSpacing` tokens (`xxs`…`xxxl`). No magic numbers for padding,
  gaps or radii.
- Every interactive target must be at least
  `AppBreakpoints.minTouchTarget` (48dp) on every side. Prefer
  `IconButton` / `ListTile` / `InkWell` over raw `GestureDetector` on a
  small child.
- On short viewports (`context.isShortViewport`, height < 480) drop
  decorative vertical space rather than let content overflow. `Gap` already
  shrinks itself by 25% in that case.

## Text and overflow

- Never assume a string fits. Use `maxLines` + `TextOverflow.ellipsis`, or
  wrap the critical number in a `FittedBox(fit: BoxFit.scaleDown)`.
- Respect the user's text scale. Do not hard-code `textScaler:
  TextScaler.noScaling` except for emoji / icon glyphs that must stay inside
  a fixed circle.
- The app clamps text scale to `0.85…1.6` in `CurrencyExchangeApp` — do not
  lower the ceiling further without a documented reason.
- Prefer `Wrap` over `Row` when children can overflow horizontally
  (badges, chips, meta rows).

## Lists vs grids

- Phone (`compact`): single-column list.
- Tablet and up: `SliverGrid` with `context.gridColumns` (2 / 3).
- Grid cells must use **`mainAxisExtent`** (fixed height), never
  `childAspectRatio`. Aspect ratios clip the moment the user scales text up.
- If a card can stack its identity + figures (narrow cell), the
  `mainAxisExtent` must be tall enough for the stacked layout.

## Orientation and keyboard

- Do not lock orientation. Landscape must work.
- Forms (none today) must scroll when the keyboard opens —
  `resizeToAvoidBottomInset: true` on the `Scaffold`.

## Loading, empty, error

- Loading placeholders must reserve the **same space** as the real content
  (shimmer skeletons). A spinner that collapses to a dot and then jumps is
  not acceptable — especially for the chart, where the spec requires shimmer.
- Error and empty states must still be pull-to-refreshable
  (`AlwaysScrollableScrollPhysics` on the host scroll view).
- Offline is a first-class state: show the cached data **and** an
  `OfflineNotice` with a relative timestamp. Never silently show stale
  numbers as if they were live.

## Accessibility

- Colour is never the only signal. Rate trends carry an arrow icon, a `+`/`−`
  sign **and** a `Semantics` label — green/red alone is not enough.
- Hit targets, contrast and text scale are part of responsive design, not a
  separate checklist.

## Checklist before opening a PR that touches UI

- [ ] Runs without overflow on a 320dp-wide phone
- [ ] Runs without overflow at text scale 1.6
- [ ] Looks intentional on a 1024dp tablet (grid or side-by-side, not a
      stretched phone layout)
- [ ] Content is capped by `ResponsiveCenter` / `ResponsiveSliverCenter`
- [ ] Loading state is a shimmer skeleton, not a bare spinner
- [ ] Error / empty / offline states are handled and still refreshable
- [ ] No new magic spacing numbers — used `AppSpacing`
- [ ] No `Platform.isX` checks for layout
